6223 0 obj
  << /ActualText (After developing the phone\055first automotive HMI and its interactive mobile interface\054 I conducted a second round of testing with eight participants from my earlier research\056\n\nThe feedback was encouraging\054 with participants appreciating the simplified interface\054 direct use of personal smartphones and the distinction between Park and Drive modes\056 However\054 testing also revealed areas that require further consideration before the concept can become a practical solution\056)
     /K [ 109 ]
     /P 6224 0 R
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

6224 0 obj
  << /K [ 6223 0 R ]
     /P 6237 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

6225 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Key User Insights \055\n\n01 \204 Device Compatibility \046 Accessibility\nPhone sizes\054 mounting adjustments\054 visibility at night and text readability emerged as important considerations\056 A universal\054 adjustable mounting mechanism and adaptable interface sizing would be necessary\056\n\n02 \204 Driving Safety \046 Interaction\nParticipants appreciated the Park and Drive separation and the restricted driving interface\056 Voice assistance should remain accessible throughout the journey\054 while complex browsing and other distracting interactions should remain restricted\056\n\n03 \204 Vehicle Integration\nReliable integration with the vehicle\047s microphone\054 speakers\054 rear camera\054 360\260 camera and other systems needs further exploration\056 Emergency calling and favourite contacts were also suggested as useful additions\056\n\n04 \204 Interface Simplicity\nThe minimal interface and direct use of personal applications were positively received\056 Participants appreciated avoiding unnecessary Android Auto\055style intermediary interactions and having only essential functions readily accessible\056\n\n05 \204 Practicality \046 Cost\nThe phone\055first approach was recognised as a potential way to reduce dedicated infotainment hardware\054 screen duplication and manufacturing complexity\056 A magnetic mounting and charging mechanism was also proposed\056\n\n06 \204 Adoption \046 Market Suitability\nFeedback revealed that not every driver would prefer a screenless dashboard\056 Some participants valued the permanence and larger viewing area of a built\055in infotainment display\054 suggesting that the concept could work better as an alternative configuration rather than a universal replacement\056)
     /K [ 110 ]
     /P 6226 0 R
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

6226 0 obj
  << /K [ 6225 0 R ]
     /P 6237 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

6227 0 obj
  << /ActualText (WHAT THE TESTING REVEALED\nThe second round of testing indicated that the phone\055first HMI is a promising alternative to conventional infotainment systems\056 Participants appreciated its minimal interface\054 direct use of personal applications\054 simplified driving mode and separation of essential vehicle controls from digital interactions\056\n\nAt the same time\054 the feedback highlighted practical challenges around device compatibility\054 screen visibility\054 vehicle integration and user preferences\056\nThe concept received positive usability feedback\054 but its success depends on making the experience adaptable to different devices\054 drivers and vehicle environments\056)
     /K [ 111 ]
     /P 6228 0 R
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

6228 0 obj
  << /K [ 6227 0 R ]
     /P 6237 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

6229 0 obj
  << /ActualText (DESIGN REFINEMENTS IDENTIFIED\nBased on the second round of testing\054 the following improvements were identified\072\nExplore a universal\054 adjustable and secure smartphone mounting mechanism\056\nIntroduce adaptable text sizing and automatic day\057night display behaviour\056\nKeep voice\055assistant access consistently available throughout the drive\056\nExplore emergency calling and favourite\055contact shortcuts\056\nEnsure reliable integration with vehicle cameras\054 microphone and audio systems\056\nInvestigate optional instrument\055cluster integration for selected media information\056\nConsider the phone\055first interface as an alternative to conventional built\055in infotainment rather than assuming it suits every driver\056\nThese refinements provide a direction for further prototyping and usability validation\056)
     /K [ 112 ]
     /P 6230 0 R
     /Pg 6005 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

